**Step 1 – Find the original speed**

The train covers 240 km in 4 h.

\[
\text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}}
              = \frac{240\ \text{km}}{4\ \text{h}}
              = 60\ \text{km/h}
\]

So the train’s original speed is **60 km/h**.

---

**Step 2 – Increase the speed by 20 km/h**

\[
\text{New speed} = 60\ \text{km/h} + 20\ \text{km/h}
                 = 80\ \text{km/h}
\]

---

**Step 3 – Compute the new travel time for the same distance**

\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
            = \frac{240\ \text{km}}{80\ \text{km/h}}
            = 3\ \text{h}
\]

---

### Result
With the speed increased by 20 km/h, the train will take **3 hours** to travel the 240 km.
